1
00:00:00,500 --> 00:00:06,006
We're Team Zen, and this is our Smart India Hackathon 2026 entry.

2
00:00:06,006 --> 00:00:09,225
Problem statement SIH26168, from ISRO:

3
00:00:09,225 --> 00:00:15,070
AI and machine learning based dead reckoning for seamless navigation.

4
00:00:15,769 --> 00:00:21,890
A trekker from Kerala once spent four days lost in a Karnataka forest, with no signal to guide her out.

5
00:00:22,189 --> 00:00:24,350
The same gap hits ordinary roads.

6
00:00:24,350 --> 00:00:28,868
An ambulance drops into an underground ramp and the blue dot freezes.

7
00:00:28,868 --> 00:00:34,499
A delivery rider enters a lane boxed in by tall buildings and the app loses the alley.

8
00:00:34,799 --> 00:00:41,049
Most vehicles in India have no inertial backup, so when GPS drops, navigation just stops.

9
00:00:41,750 --> 00:00:42,342
Our idea:

10
00:00:42,342 --> 00:00:46,291
use the accelerometer and gyroscope every phone already has.

11
00:00:46,291 --> 00:00:51,820
Adding up their readings drifts within seconds, because tiny sensor errors compound.

12
00:00:52,120 --> 00:00:54,637
So we don't ask a network to guess position.

13
00:00:54,637 --> 00:01:04,019
A small network learns only how wrong the sensors are, exact physics does the rest, and the whole chain trains on the drift percentage the problem statement grades.

14
00:01:04,719 --> 00:01:06,236
Here's the whole system.

15
00:01:06,236 --> 00:01:13,250
Offline, we train in PyTorch and export an ONNX model into the Android app, which runs everything on the phone.

16
00:01:13,549 --> 00:01:21,000
We train on the public IO-VNBD and comma2k19 datasets, plus our own drives from Jalandhar.

17
00:01:21,299 --> 00:01:27,679
On the phone, ten times a second, sensor readings go through calibration and into the fusion engine.

18
00:01:27,979 --> 00:01:30,158
While GPS is good, we trust it.

19
00:01:30,158 --> 00:01:34,798
When it drops, blackout mode runs the model in five second chunks.

20
00:01:34,798 --> 00:01:40,139
When GPS returns, we blend back over two seconds, so the marker never jumps.

21
00:01:40,439 --> 00:01:46,799
A map matcher then snaps the estimate onto the real road graph, because a car can't drive through a building.

22
00:01:47,500 --> 00:01:52,306
On a real recorded highway drive, we cut GPS for thirty seconds.

23
00:01:52,306 --> 00:01:59,665
The red trail is the model alone, and it ends with 2.9 percent drift, under the 10 percent target.

24
00:01:59,665 --> 00:02:02,820
When GPS returns, the jump is zero metres.

25
00:02:03,519 --> 00:02:04,774
Now the honest part.

26
00:02:04,774 --> 00:02:10,669
Our urban results were stuck near 62 percent drift, and five different fixes didn't move them.

27
00:02:10,969 --> 00:02:12,726
So we checked the data.

28
00:02:12,726 --> 00:02:22,733
In IO-VNBD the speed column says kilometres per hour but is really metres per second, so every speed label was 3.6 times too small.

29
00:02:22,733 --> 00:02:25,560
The GPS labels were also stair steps.

30
00:02:25,860 --> 00:02:35,050
After fixing both and retraining, urban drift on the same test set fell from 77 percent mean to 19, with a median of 11.2.

31
00:02:35,350 --> 00:02:38,453
On highway driving the median is 5.8 percent.

32
00:02:38,453 --> 00:02:41,487
Urban stop and go is still the hardest case.

33
00:02:41,487 --> 00:02:46,660
Next, we ship the retrained model in the app and re-validate it end to end.

34
00:02:47,360 --> 00:02:48,712
It's feasible today:

35
00:02:48,712 --> 00:02:56,150
standard phone sensors, fully on-device at ten hertz, testable with or without GPS, and no new infrastructure.

36
00:02:56,450 --> 00:03:01,793
Navigation keeps working in tunnels, parking and dense streets with no sudden jump.

37
00:03:01,793 --> 00:03:07,330
That helps ambulances, fleets and delivery riders, and it scales to robots and drones.

38
00:03:08,030 --> 00:03:11,319
We build on published research, with references on screen.

39
00:03:11,319 --> 00:03:12,680
Thank you from Team Zen.

